-- title:
--   Farkas' lemma for a mixed system of inequalities and equations
-- statement:
--   Let $E$ be a finite-dimensional real vector space, let $a_i$ and $e_j$ be finitely many linear functionals on $E$, and let $b_i, d_j \in \mathbb{R}$. Then exactly one of the two alternatives holds:
--
--   $$
--   \exists\, x \in E \ \text{ with } \ \langle a_i, x\rangle \le b_i \ \ \forall i \quad\text{and}\quad \langle e_j, x\rangle = d_j \ \ \forall j,
--   $$
--
--   or there are multipliers $y \ge 0$ and $z$ (free) with
--
--   $$
--   \sum_i y_i\, a_i + \sum_j z_j\, e_j = 0 \quad\text{and}\quad y \cdot b + z \cdot d < 0 .
--   $$
--
--   Formally, the statement is the equivalence between *infeasibility* of the first system and
--   existence of the second.
--
--   This is the theorem of the alternative that linear programming duality is built from. The
--   second alternative is a **certificate of infeasibility**: a nonnegative combination of the
--   inequalities together with an arbitrary combination of the equations that produces the
--   contradiction $0 \le y\cdot b + z \cdot d < 0$. The form stated here is the one an application
--   actually needs -- inequalities and equations together, functionals rather than a matrix, so
--   that it applies verbatim to a space of matrices, of polynomials, or of measures.
--
--   The proof is separation: infeasibility says a certain point lies outside the convex cone
--   generated by the data, the cone is closed because it is finitely generated, and a strictly
--   separating functional is exactly the multiplier vector.
--
--   **Formalization Note** The functionals live in `Module.Dual ℝ E`, so $y$ and $z$ enter the
--   conclusion as the coefficients of a vanishing combination *in the dual space*; the dot products
--   $y \cdot b$ and $z \cdot d$ are `Matrix.dotProduct`. The closedness input is
--   `ConvexCone.isClosed_finite_cone`.
-- source:
--   Carried as an internal support lemma, with this exact statement and proof, in twelve accepted submissions across ten missions of the OR Formalization Drive (e.g. https://prove2.me/submissions/3c55ced4-4944-4de2-ba43-8110d2682db1); published here once so that it can be imported. Farkas' lemma; see Schrijver, Theory of Linear and Integer Programming, Corollary 7.1d, or Rockafellar, Convex Analysis, §22.

import Mathlib

open Matrix

variable {E : Type*} [AddCommGroup E] [Module ℝ E] [FiniteDimensional ℝ E]
variable {ι κ : Type*} [Fintype ι] [Fintype κ]

namespace LP

theorem farkas (a : ι → Module.Dual ℝ E) (b : ι → ℝ) (e : κ → Module.Dual ℝ E) (d : κ → ℝ) :
    (¬ ∃ x : E, (∀ i, a i x ≤ b i) ∧ ∀ j, e j x = d j) ↔
      ∃ (y : ι → ℝ) (z : κ → ℝ), 0 ≤ y ∧
        ∑ i, y i • a i + ∑ j, z j • e j = 0 ∧ y ⬝ᵥ b + z ⬝ᵥ d < 0 := by sorry

end LP
